1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,500
Section 4

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,614
In my presentation, I'm going to talk about coffee,

3
00:00:05,614 --> 00:00:10,150
and its importance both in economic and social terms.

4
00:00:10,150 --> 00:00:13,337
We think it was first drunk in the Arab world,

5
00:00:13,337 --> 00:00:19,951
but there's hardly any documentary evidence of it before the 1500s, although of course

6
00:00:19,951 --> 00:00:23,900
that doesn't mean that people didn't know about it before then.

7
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:30,039
However, there is evidence that coffee was originally gathered from bushes growing wild in

8
00:00:30,039 --> 00:00:33,350
Ethiopia, in the northeast of Africa.

9
00:00:33,350 --> 00:00:37,888
In the early sixteenth century, it was being bought by traders,

10
00:00:37,888 --> 00:00:43,350
and gradually its use as a drink spread throughout the Middle East.

11
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:49,248
It's also known that in 1522, in the Turkish city of Constantinople,

12
00:00:49,248 --> 00:00:54,805
which was the centre of the Ottoman Empire, the court physician approved its use

13
00:00:54,805 --> 00:00:56,450
as a medicine.

14
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:01,702
By the mid-1500s, coffee bushes were being cultivated in the Yemen

15
00:01:01,702 --> 00:01:07,935
and for the next hundred years this region produced most of the coffee drunk in Africa

16
00:01:07,935 --> 00:01:10,000
and the Arab world.

17
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,850
What's particularly interesting about coffee is its effect on social life.

18
00:01:15,850 --> 00:01:18,043
It was rarely drunk at home,

19
00:01:18,043 --> 00:01:22,450
but instead people went to coffee houses to drink it.

20
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:28,524
These people, usually men, would meet to drink coffee and chat about issues of

21
00:01:28,524 --> 00:01:29,550
the day.

22
00:01:29,550 --> 00:01:35,230
But at the time, this chance to share ideas and opinions was seen as

23
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:38,100
something that was potentially dangerous,

24
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:45,279
and in 1623 the ruler of Constantinople demanded the destruction of all the coffee

25
00:01:45,279 --> 00:01:50,612
houses in the city, although after his death many new ones opened,

26
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:53,500
and coffee consumption continued.

27
00:01:53,500 --> 00:01:57,512
In the seventeenth century, coffee drinking spread to Europe,

28
00:01:57,512 --> 00:02:04,433
and here too coffee shops became places where ordinary people, nearly always men, could

29
00:02:04,433 --> 00:02:06,800
meet to exchange ideas.

30
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:12,269
Because of this, some people said that these places performed a similar function to

31
00:02:12,269 --> 00:02:14,400
universities.

32
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:19,591
The opportunity they provided for people to meet together outside their own homes

33
00:02:19,591 --> 00:02:24,863
and to discuss the topics of the day had an enormous impact on social

34
00:02:24,863 --> 00:02:25,183
life,

35
00:02:25,183 --> 00:02:32,800
and many social movements and political developments had their origins in coffee house discussions.

36
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:38,679
In the late 1600s, the Yemeni monopoly on coffee production broke down

37
00:02:38,679 --> 00:02:45,100
and coffee production started to spread around the world, helped by European colonisation.

38
00:02:45,100 --> 00:02:50,503
Europeans set up coffee plantations in Indonesia and the Caribbean

39
00:02:50,503 --> 00:02:54,700
and production of coffee in the colonies skyrocketed.

40
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:58,210
Different types of coffee were produced in different areas,

41
00:02:58,210 --> 00:03:03,664
and it's interesting that the names given to these different types, like Mocha

42
00:03:03,664 --> 00:03:08,477
or Java coffee, were often taken from the port they were shipped to Europe

43
00:03:08,477 --> 00:03:09,400
from.

44
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:13,566
But if you look at the labour system in the different colonies, there were

45
00:03:13,566 --> 00:03:15,700
some significant differences.

46
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:21,983
In Brazil and the various Caribbean colonies, coffee was grown in huge plantations

47
00:03:21,983 --> 00:03:26,400
and the workers there were almost all slaves.

48
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:28,887
But this wasn't the same in all colonies;

49
00:03:28,887 --> 00:03:31,052
for example in Java,

50
00:03:31,052 --> 00:03:35,123
which had been colonised by the Dutch, the peasants grew coffee

51
00:03:35,123 --> 00:03:37,991
and passed a proportion of this on to the Dutch,

52
00:03:37,991 --> 00:03:41,500
so it was used as a means of taxation.

53
00:03:41,500 --> 00:03:47,939
But whatever system was used, under the European powers of the eighteenth century, coffee

54
00:03:47,939 --> 00:03:51,550
production was very closely linked to colonisation.

55
00:03:51,550 --> 00:03:57,504
Coffee was grown in ever-increasing quantities to satisfy the growing demand from Europe,

56
00:03:57,504 --> 00:04:01,272
and it became nearly as important as sugar production,

57
00:04:01,272 --> 00:04:04,700
which was grown under very similar conditions.

58
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:09,754
However, coffee prices were not yet low enough for people to drink it regularly

59
00:04:09,754 --> 00:04:10,336
at home,

60
00:04:10,336 --> 00:04:15,069
so most coffee consumption still took place in public coffee houses

61
00:04:15,069 --> 00:04:18,800
and it still remained something of a luxury item.

62
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:22,972
In Britain, however, a new drink was introduced from China,

63
00:04:22,972 --> 00:04:28,989
and started to become popular, gradually taking over from coffee, although at first it was

64
00:04:28,989 --> 00:04:32,700
so expensive that only the upper classes could afford it.

65
00:04:32,700 --> 00:04:34,829
This was tea,

66
00:04:34,829 --> 00:04:39,550
and by the late 1700s it was being widely drunk.

67
00:04:39,550 --> 00:04:40,512
However,

68
00:04:40,512 --> 00:04:47,247
when the USA gained independence from Britain in 1776, they identified this drink with

69
00:04:47,247 --> 00:04:47,648
Britain,

70
00:04:47,648 --> 00:04:53,400
and coffee remained the preferred drink in the USA, as it still is today.

71
00:04:53,400 --> 00:05:00,550
So, by the early nineteenth century, coffee was already being widely produced and consumed.

72
00:05:00,550 --> 00:05:07,000
But during this century, production boomed and coffee prices started to fall.

73
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,483
This was partly because new types of transportation had been developed which were cheaper

74
00:05:12,483 --> 00:05:14,150
and more efficient.

75
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:18,971
So now, working people could afford to buy coffee - it wasn't just a

76
00:05:18,971 --> 00:05:21,000
drink for the middle classes.

77
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:24,981
And this was at a time when large parts of Europe were starting to

78
00:05:24,981 --> 00:05:26,650
work in industries.

79
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:30,846
And sometimes this meant their work didn't stop when it got dark:

80
00:05:30,846 --> 00:05:34,500
they might have to continue throughout the night.

81
00:05:34,500 --> 00:05:39,504
So, the use of coffee as a stimulant became important - it wasn't just

82
00:05:39,504 --> 00:05:42,700
a drink people drank in the morning, for breakfast.

83
00:05:42,700 --> 00:05:46,300
There were also changes in cultivation ...
